{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act criminal record screening limits","requirement":"A housing provider may not inquire about an applicant's criminal record before a conditional offer, and may never consider arrests without conviction, expunged, sealed, vacated, pardoned or juvenile records. After an offer, only listed recent convictions may be weighed by individualized assessment.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental dwelling units offered for rent by a housing provider, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises","unit_limit":4}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["“Rental dwelling unit” means a dwelling unit offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."]},"exemptions":"Dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units; pre-offer consideration of methamphetamine manufacture convictions in federally assisted housing and lifetime sex offender registration.","penalty":"Monetary penalty up to $1,000 for a first violation, $5,000 for a second within five years, and $10,000 for two or more within seven years; the director may order cease-and-desist, compliance reporting, a conditional offer or similar unit, or payment to the applicant up to $1,000.","effective_date":"2022-01-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"P.L. 2021, c. 110","quoted_span":"A housing provider shall not require an applicant to complete any housing application that includes any inquiries regarding an applicant’s criminal record prior to the provision of a conditional offer","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act application fee disclosure and refund","requirement":"Before accepting any application fee, a housing provider must disclose in writing whether it reviews criminal history and that the applicant may submit evidence of inaccuracies or rehabilitation. The director may require the fee be returned if the violation denied or withdrew a conditional offer.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental dwelling units offered for rent by a housing provider, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises","unit_limit":4}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["“Rental dwelling unit” means a dwelling unit offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."]},"exemptions":"Dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","penalty":"Monetary penalty up to $1,000 for a first violation, $5,000 for a second within five years, and $10,000 for two or more within seven years; the director may require the application fee be returned.","effective_date":"2022-01-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"P.L. 2021, c. 110","quoted_span":"Prior to accepting any application fee, a housing provider shall disclose in writing to the applicant: (1) Whether the eligibility criteria of the housing provider include the review and consideration of criminal history","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act eviction after successful appeal","requirement":"If an appeal succeeds and a court overturns the director's order, the resulting determination that the housing provider did not violate the act is grounds to evict the former applicant who resides in the unit because of that order, on at least 45 days' notice.","key_value":"45 days' notice","coverage_conditions":"Applies to a former applicant who resides in a rental dwelling unit owned by the housing provider as the result of the director's order in a violation case.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises","unit_limit":4}],"per_tenancy":"Former applicant residing in the unit as the result of the director's order","coverage_quotes":["“Rental dwelling unit” means a dwelling unit offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."]},"exemptions":"Dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2022-01-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-63","quoted_span":"shall be grounds for the housing provider to evict the former applicant if that person resides in a rental dwelling unit owned by the housing provider as the result of the director’s order, so long as the housing provider provides the applicant with at least 45 days’ notice prior to the eviction","interaction":"Operates notwithstanding the Anti-Eviction Act, P.L.1974, c.49 (C.2A:18-61.1 et seq.).","relations":[],"confidence":0.85,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D065-01","n_rules":3,"note":null}

